---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/ArticleHeader.astro';
import StreamReveal from '../../components/StreamReveal.astro';

/**
 * Preview scaffold. The copy belongs in a Sanity `post` as markdownBody with
 * {{stream-reveal}} on its own line, which renders <StreamReveal /> in
 * src/pages/posts/[slug].astro. This page exists so the piece and the component
 * can be read and measured on a real build before that write happens, then it
 * comes out.
 */
const TITLE = 'The stream is one view of the answer';
const LEDE =
  'The model gives you text a few characters at a time, which is a fact about the model and not a rule about the screen. Six drawings of the same answer, with the layout cost of each measured in the page.';
const MINUTES = 3;
---

<Base
  title={TITLE}
  description={LEDE}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Writing', path: '/work' },
    { name: TITLE, path: '/notes/one-view-of-the-answer' },
  ]}
>
  <article class="article">
    <ArticleHeader
      title={TITLE}
      lede={LEDE}
      meta={['7 November 2026', `${MINUTES} min read`]}
      backHref="/work?type=writing"
      backLabel="All writing"
    />

    <div class="prose article__body">
      <p>
        A chat box on a product I have been working in streams its answer one character at a time. I
        have watched maybe a dozen people use it now and they all do the same thing, which is try to
        read a sentence that is not there yet. The word they are on finishes itself while their eye
        has moved on, the line jumps when the wrap changes, and roughly a third of the answer gets
        read a second time once it stops moving.
      </p>

      <p>
        I assumed for a while that this was a property of the model, something you put up with in
        exchange for the first words coming out fast. The generation is a token at a time and a token
        is three or four characters, so the text really does come out in bits. What it does not
        decide is how those bits get drawn. The same answer could go onto the page as a table, a map,
        three paragraphs or one sentence, and picking between those is a layout decision made in the
        browser, not something the model has an opinion about.
      </p>

      <h2>What gives it away</h2>

      <p>
        The speed is the first thing people blame and it is not the problem. A machine printing a
        hundred tokens a second looks just as machine made as a slow one. What gives it away is
        smaller. Partial words. A cadence so even it is obviously a clock. And a paragraph that moves
        while somebody is reading it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The first two are easy to live with. The third costs a reader their place in the sentence,
        and it stops the moment the paragraph is at full size before anything is drawn.
      </p>

      <StreamReveal />

      <h2>Nothing on screen for a second</h2>

      <p>
        If you wait for the answer to finish and then draw it, the paragraph is at its final size
        from the first frame and nothing moves underneath anyone. Every word is in the page and
        invisible, and the animation only brings the opacity up. The eye gets a target that does not
        move.
      </p>

      <p>
        The cost is a second with nothing on it. A second of nothing does read longer than a second
        of watching text fill in, which is the honest case for streaming and the reason everything
        does it. It is a real case, and it only holds while the wait is longer than the thing being
        waited for.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the line sits</h2>

      <p>
        Under about two seconds there is nothing to cover, so spend the time on the drawing instead.
        Past about eight seconds the wait is the problem and text filling in is the only honest
        signal that something is still happening.
      </p>

      <p>
        In between, the answer is usually long enough to have structure, and structure is what a
        stream is worst at. A stream produces a single shape, a paragraph getting longer downward, so
        a comparison arrives as prose whether or not a table was the right answer. Once the text is
        data before it is painted, the layout gets decided first: a heading, then rows, or two
        columns, or a map with one sentence underneath it, and what the reader sees is an animation
        over a page that already exists.
      </p>

      <p>
        The one I would actually ship is the last one, which hardly animates at all. It holds the
        answer, keeps the space the answer will take, says how long it has been working, and then
        paints the finished thing in one go. The layout is decided before anyone sees a word, so the
        reader gets a page instead of a performance of a page. Most of what I built here is the
        animation, and the animation is the part I would cut.
      </p>

      <p>
        Chunks are what I would use when the wait is too long to hide. Reading does not happen a word
        at a time. Skimming a page I take in about a clause before I know what it said, somewhere
        near the commas, and the meaning lands whether or not the rest of the words have shown up.
        Chunks match that. Words feel like being read to. Characters feel like a printer.
      </p>

      <p>
        The case I have not worked out is a four second answer with three sections in it, where
        holding the whole thing feels long and streaming it as prose throws away structure you know
        is coming. Probably the heading goes first, then each section as a block, and the movement is
        line to line instead of character to character. Which is still a stream, just not a stream of
        text.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .article {
    margin-inline: auto;
    max-width: var(--reading-width);
  }

  .article__body {
    margin-top: var(--spacing-10);
  }
</style>
